<h1>Key Concepts</h1>

<p>
    Understanding these core concepts will help you work more efficiently in Quickfire. These ideas appear throughout the system, so getting comfortable with them now will save you time later.
</p>

<!-- IMAGE PLACEHOLDER: A visual diagram or collage showing the main concepts: tasks attached to a renewal card, an agent button, a form being edited, and a file attachment. Use icons or simple illustrations to represent each concept. -->

<h2>Tasks</h2>

<p>
    Tasks are simple to-do items that help you track work that needs to be done. In Quickfire, tasks are always connected to something specific—usually a renewal or a proposal—so all the related information stays together.
</p>

<p>
    When you create a task, it becomes part of that renewal or proposal's workspace. This means:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>All tasks for a renewal are visible when you open that renewal</li>
    <li>You can add notes, files, and activity history that's tied to the task</li>
    <li>The task's history is preserved, so you can see who did what and when</li>
    <li>Tasks can have goal dates that help you stay on schedule</li>
</ul>

<p>
    Tasks aren't just standalone reminders—they're part of a bigger picture that helps you manage complex workflows like policy renewals from start to finish.
</p>

