<h2>Files, Forms, and Attachments</h2>

<p>
    Quickfire handles different types of documents in different ways. Understanding the difference will help you choose the right tool for the job.
</p>

<h3>Attachments</h3>

<p>
    Attachments are files that are stored in Quickfire and can be shared or previewed. Think of them as documents you've uploaded that are now part of the system. You can:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Attach them to clients, renewals, policies, or other records</li>
    <li>Preview them directly in Quickfire (for PDFs, images, etc.)</li>
    <li>Download them when needed</li>
    <li>Share them with team members</li>
</ul>

<p>
    Attachments are great for documents that are already complete and just need to be stored and accessed.
</p>

<h3>Forms (Live PDFs)</h3>

<p>
    Forms are special PDFs that you can edit directly in Quickfire. Unlike attachments, forms are "live" PDFs that you can fill out, edit, and save multiple versions of. Key features:
</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>In-app editing</strong> – Type directly into PDF form fields without leaving Quickfire</li>
    <li><strong>Version history</strong> – Every time you save, a new version is created. The old version is preserved, so you can go back if needed</li>
    <li><strong>Non-destructive</strong> – Saving never overwrites—it always creates a new revision</li>
    <li><strong>Revert capability</strong> – If someone makes a mistake, you can reopen a previous version and continue from there</li>
</ul>

<p>
    Forms are perfect for ACORD forms, certificates, and other documents that need to be filled out and might need changes later.
</p>

<h3>Files (Links to Local Files)</h3>

<p>
    Sometimes you need to work with files that are stored on your computer's hard drive rather than in Quickfire. These are called "file links" or just "files."
</p>

<p>
    When you create a file link:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>You're creating a reference to a file on your computer or network drive</li>
    <li>Clicking the link opens the file using Windows (or your default application)</li>
    <li>You can copy the file path with one click if you need to share it or use it elsewhere</li>
    <li>The file stays where it is—Quickfire just remembers where to find it</li>
</ul>

<p>
    This is different from opening a file in a web browser. File links open files locally on your computer, which is useful for files that need to be edited in specific programs (like Word documents or Excel spreadsheets).
</p>

