<h2>Links: Web Browser vs. Local Files</h2>

<p>
    Quickfire has two different ways of opening things, and it's important to understand the difference:
</p>

<h3>Opening in a Web Browser</h3>

<p>
    When you click a link that opens in your web browser, you're viewing something through the internet. This might be:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>A webpage or portal</li>
    <li>A document stored online</li>
    <li>A web-based application</li>
</ul>

<p>
    These links work the same way as clicking a link on any website—they open in your default web browser.
</p>

<h3>Opening a File with Windows</h3>

<p>
    When you click a link that opens a file locally, Windows opens it using the program associated with that file type. For example:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>A Word document opens in Microsoft Word</li>
    <li>An Excel file opens in Microsoft Excel</li>
    <li>A PDF might open in Adobe Reader or your default PDF viewer</li>
</ul>

<p>
    The file is opened on your computer, not through a web browser. This is useful when you need to edit the file in a specific program or when the file is stored on a network drive that your computer can access directly.
</p>

